\description{
Inside \code{\link[=params]{params()}}, \code{arg(type, default)} gives a parameter of a generic or
trait method its type and, when \code{default} is given, its default, an R
expression evaluated at each call that omits the argument. Each type
constructor has a shorthand, with the constructor's arguments and then a
named \code{default}: \code{arg_double(min = 0, default = 1)} is
\code{arg(type_double(min = 0), 1)}.
}
\details{
A parameter without a default is required. \code{arg_option(type)} has the
default \code{NULL}: the argument may be omitted and is then absent;
\code{arg(option(type))} is required and may be \code{NULL}. \code{arg(default = expr)}
gives a parameter without a type a default that is itself a type, such as
\code{arg(default = GBM)}.

\code{description} is named after \code{default}: \code{arg_integer(min = 1L, default = 10L, description = "The number of folds.")}. \code{arg(description = "...")}
alone describes a parameter without a type or a default.

Each call of the generic checks and converts its typed arguments
(\code{\link[=generic]{generic()}}).

A type means the same in a parameter as in a field: \code{arg(Range)} admits a
value of exactly \code{Range}, \code{arg(subtype(Range))} also the structs that
extend it, \code{arg(dyn(Fit))} any implementor of the trait \code{Fit}, and
\code{arg(host_class("data.frame"))} an object of that class.
}
